{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>But if you remember me with you.</b> He asked of him two things: the first, that he himself remember him, and the second, that he make mention of him to Pharaoh. And on account of this it is written, ״ולא זכר שר המשקים את יוסף וישכחהו״ — 'and the chief butler did not remember Joseph, and forgot him': that is, he did not remember him for himself, and he forgot him, from making mention of him to Pharaoh. And this is the sense of ולא זכר וישכחהו — the one corresponds to ״והזכרתני אל פרעה״. And heaven forbid that Joseph the righteous should hang his trust upon the chief butler — only in HaShem alone. Rather, his intention was that the Holy One, blessed be He, had brought the chief butler to him in order to perform a miracle for him through him, for it is well known that the deliverance of the righteous by the Holy One, blessed be He, comes about through causes. And the fact that he was punished by being left there an additional two years, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded, was on account of the speech he directed to the chief butler concerning his deliverance, pressing him for the matter. And because he sought a cause in the chief butler — that he should mention him and bring him out, as he said to him, ״והזכרתני״ and ״והוצאתני״ — and it is not fitting for righteous men such as him to seek a cause, therefore he was punished for this. For he should have trusted in the Holy One, blessed be He, alone, who is the Master of all causes, that He would bring him the cause without his seeking it himself. And for a righteous man like him it was accounted a sin, for the Holy One, blessed be He, scrutinizes with the righteous even to a hair's breadth. And such is the way of the perfect righteous in their trust — that they do not seek a cause at all. And thus we find with Elijah, who stood in the wilderness lacking food and trusted in the Holy One, blessed be He, and the Holy One, blessed be He, brought him ravens there and sustained him, as it is written (I Kings 17:6): ״והעורבים מביאים לו לחם ובשר בבקר ולחם ובשר בערב״ — 'and the ravens brought him bread and flesh in the morning, and bread and flesh in the evening.' And behold, the ravens were a cause for his sustenance — whether by way of miracle, or from the table of Jehoshaphat. The Holy One, blessed be He, brought him that cause, and he did not seek it. And what emerges for us from this is that Joseph the righteous did not hang his trust upon flesh and blood at all, and the punishment was for seeking a cause."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:14.087944429Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}